; Linux x86-64 syscall-backed byte IO and process query primitives.
; Import only for cfg(os=linux, arch=x86_64). Raw syscall errors are returned
; as negative errno values in #i64; no libc or FFI calls are made here.
;
; Caller safety: every raw-pointer API is #unsafe-fn, including path strings
; and nullable output buffers. The caller supplies the kernel ABI layout,
; bounds, initialization, alignment, access rights, and exclusive/shared access
; appropriate to the operation. An errno result is not a memory-safety proof.
; Pointer-bearing records (iovec, msghdr, signal actions, etc.) include their
; nested pointers. Retained buffers/registrations must remain live until the
; kernel has finished using them, not merely until this function returns.
; Mapping/protection operations must preserve all outstanding Lim references.
; Integer-only control operations with hidden addresses or process execution
; effects also require #unsafe. See docs/syscall-coverage.md, Caller safety
; contracts, for the operation-specific obligations and safe helper boundary.
; Unsupported-target declarations preserve the same caller contract.
(#import "linux_x86_64_syscall_numbers.lim")
; Lim stack aggregates are laid out toward lower addresses. Keep nanoseconds
; first so a pointer adjusted to the low end has the Linux C timespec order.
(#struct LinuxTimespec ((nanoseconds #i64) (seconds #i64)))
